WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
BORGERSON: OK, good morning, everyone.

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:02.000
Thank you for coming to the Council's meeting
on the Arctic.

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.000
It's really terrific, and a great honor, to
have three of the country's-our nation's most

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:04.000
important leaders on this subject: Admiral
Papp, Ambassador Brzezinski, and Senator Murkowski.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.490
Their full bios are in your program, so I
won't repeat them for you here.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.420
Our plan, like most Council meetings, is to
have a 30-minute conversation, and then we'll

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.870
open it up to the floor for members to ask
questions.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:18.990
I'd like to point out for this room that this
meeting is on the record, not off the record-on

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:23.510
the record, and this is being streamed live
on the Council's website, and there are many

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:28.510
people watching, not just in D.C. but from
around the world, to this meeting.

00:00:28.510 --> 00:00:33.620
So please keep that in mind: our audience
is much larger than this room for this conversation.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:38.350
And this meeting is coming at a very important
time for not just the United States in the

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.680
Arctic, but the Arctic.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:48.030
Yesterday's Washington Post ran an article
about sea ice melt in May, which was not just

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:54.770
historic, it was off the charts in terms of
setting records of the rate at which the ice

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:55.770
is melting.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:59.910
Today's Washington Post had an article about
how hot Alaska is.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:04.949
The whole Earth is warming, but it was the
hottest spring in the history of Alaska, I

00:01:04.949 --> 00:01:09.890
think the article said 8 degrees warmer than
average this spring, and Alaskans are seeing

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:13.950
really historic warmer temperatures.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:20.770
The U.S. is chair of the Arctic Council, and
we have with us former Coast Guard Commandant

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:27.270
Admiral Papp, who is the-our country's first
special representative to the Arctic, as well

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:32.360
as a person chairing an executive steering
committee at the White House showing the seriousness

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:38.009
with which our country is taking this moment,
and of our leadership of the Arctic Council,

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:42.770
and also sort of this historic moment in the
region itself.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:48.829
We have an Alaskan with us, who's been a leader
on this issue much longer than any of us on

00:01:48.829 --> 00:01:53.320
the panel who aren't Alaskans, and knows this
issue closely, and has been an advocate for

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:59.580
not just Alaskans but American foreign policy
in the Arctic for many years.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:03.980
And when you add in the Law of the Sea, with
what's happening in the South China Seas (sic)

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:08.420
and how that might relate to the Arctic Ocean,
Asian interests in the Arctic, new members

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:15.520
in the Arctic Council, China sending icebreakers
through, cruise ships coming this summer transiting

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:23.480
the Northwest Passage, Shell and oil and gas,
and a long list of other things, it is a really

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.680
interesting and unique time.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.730
And I would add maybe just as a footnote to
that, the Council on Foreign Relations is

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:34.340
becoming more involved in the Arctic, which
I'm pleased about as a former fellow here,

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:36.160
having worked on the Arctic before.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:41.090
And we have a task force not related to this
meeting, but that has convened, that endeavors

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:45.870
to make recommendations for U.S. foreign policy
in the Arctic for the next administration.

00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:52.500
So, with that introduction, if I may ask the
first question to you, Ambassador Brzezinski.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.480
Give us your view, if you would, please, of
how foreign policy is going in the Arctic,

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.290
and specifically how you define national security
in the Arctic, if you would.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:02.459
BRZEZINSKI: Thank you, Scott.

00:03:02.459 --> 00:03:07.739
And thank you to the Council on Foreign Relations
for hosting this session, and for also convening

00:03:07.739 --> 00:03:11.310
a task force on U.S. policy on the Arctic.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:18.580
It is absolutely timely, given the changes
in the Arctic, for a clear-headed assessment

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:22.890
on the international ramifications of the
Arctic issue.

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:30.069
And defining national security on the Arctic,
I take the broad approach.

00:03:30.069 --> 00:03:36.760
Of course, national security as it pertains
to the Arctic focuses on domain awareness,

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:38.349
what others are doing.

00:03:38.349 --> 00:03:41.530
We're not indifferent or passive to that.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:47.440
But it also involves energy security-energy
security for the world, but also for the people

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:48.629
of the Arctic.

00:03:48.629 --> 00:03:55.409
It involves food security-food security as
it pertains to subsistence communities in

00:03:55.409 --> 00:03:56.409
the Arctic.

00:03:56.409 --> 00:04:03.269
Water and sanitation is one of the central
organizing challenges in rural Alaska, access

00:04:03.269 --> 00:04:04.769
to clean water.

00:04:04.769 --> 00:04:08.730
So I define national security as it pertains
to the Arctic broadly.

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:18.329
And I want to emphasize that the Arctic is
simultaneously a strategic challenge and a

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:24.310
human challenge-a strategic challenge pertaining
to the different definitions of security that

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:28.020
I just mentioned, but also a-human challenges.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:38.080
Because there are millions of people-native
and tribal people, and Americans, Scandinavians,

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:44.160
Russians-who live in the Arctic area whose
context is changing dramatically because of

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.180
the impact of climate change.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:50.960
It's important for us as an Arctic nation
to stay ahead of that.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.500
And I'm proud to work with my two colleagues
on the podium here, Senator Murkowski and

00:04:55.500 --> 00:05:02.210
Admiral Papp, because there really in the
last year or so has been an ascension of focus

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:07.630
on the Arctic issue in the federal family,
covering the range of issues that pertain

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:13.520
to the Arctic-native and tribal health, water
and sanitation, energy, state and local, international,

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.780
and in other dimensions as well.

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:16.920
BORGERSON: Perfect.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:22.060
And I should have mentioned in my introductory
comments-and I'm dying to ask-President Obama

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:26.320
I do believe was the first sitting U.S. president
to visit the Arctic while in office.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:27.320
Is that correct?

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:28.320
BRZEZINSKI: That is correct.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.160
I was actually really surprised, Scott, when
I began this job last August, that no previous

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:36.710
American president had put his two feet on
Arctic soil.

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:40.970
And the president went to the Arctic last
September for several days.

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:43.890
Three days was the timing of his trip in total.

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:47.400
That's expensive real estate on the presidential
calendar.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.290
But he went there for two reasons.

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:54.290
First of all, to bear witness to what is happening
there with the people of the Arctic.

00:05:54.290 --> 00:06:00.260
And so he went up to northern Alaska, to the
Arctic area, to bear witness firsthand.

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:09.289
But the higher meaning of the president's
visit to the Arctic was that the looming crisis

00:06:09.289 --> 00:06:15.710
in the Arctic is a tangible preview of the
looming crisis of the global condition.

00:06:15.710 --> 00:06:22.480
Meaning this: The Arctic is a foreshadow,
is an augury, to what will happen elsewhere.

00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:26.669
It's heating up more quickly than anywhere
else on Earth.

00:06:26.669 --> 00:06:33.710
But coastal erosion, changing weather patterns,
resilience issues are what will affect us

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:37.680
all looking forward, and it's important for
us to get ahead of that.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:38.690
BORGERSON: Thank you.

00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:42.080
Admiral Papp, how is chairmanship of the U.S.
Arctic Council going?

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:45.800
PAPP: I think it's going extremely well.

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:48.259
Almost all our objectives are being met.

00:06:48.259 --> 00:06:50.740
We have just gone over the halfway point.

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:56.130
We started out with what I have referred to
as a very balanced and ambitious program.

00:06:56.130 --> 00:06:59.370
I didn't come up with those terms myself,
although I did want a balanced program.

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:03.460
I wanted something that would bring all interests
in, whether you're an environmentalist, involved

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:07.740
in security, or industry.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:11.660
We wanted everybody to see something within
our chairmanship program that would draw their

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:13.590
interest and get them involved.

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:16.949
And I think we've been successful there.

00:07:16.949 --> 00:07:22.979
The ambitious part, everybody that I spoke
to as we briefed our chairmanship program,

00:07:22.979 --> 00:07:27.740
one of their first comments was, wow, that's
rather ambitious-except for one person: John

00:07:27.740 --> 00:07:28.849
Kerry.

00:07:28.849 --> 00:07:32.569
He's always asked me, is there something more
that we can be doing?

00:07:32.569 --> 00:07:34.949
Can't we find other things to be done?

00:07:34.949 --> 00:07:38.449
And so he's been great to push us along.

00:07:38.449 --> 00:07:40.970
We are well long into our program.

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:45.789
But I think more importantly, going back to
something you just asked about, while we had

00:07:45.789 --> 00:07:51.240
our chairmanship program which involved about
a dozen or more individual projects, we had

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:52.780
some strategic goals.

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:57.580
One thing was to make the Arctic Council a
stronger international forum.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:02.220
And I think we're well on our way doing that,
reviewing our procedures, strengthening the

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:07.920
Secretariat in Troms�, and doing a review
of our observers.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:13.820
The second thing strategically, we wanted
to introduce long-term objectives.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:19.080
It's my opinion that all too often-and it
probably comes because I've always been involved

00:08:19.080 --> 00:08:23.949
in working strategic issues and looking into
the future-the Arctic Council focused on that

00:08:23.949 --> 00:08:25.009
two-year chairmanship.

00:08:25.009 --> 00:08:30.690
At the end of two years, we do a declaration,
we raise the flags and cheer the accomplishments

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:33.820
of the last two years, and then you start
over with a new chairmanship.

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:38.260
So we want something that's going to transcend
our chairmanship, and we've been working very

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:44.120
closely with Finland-with the next chair-to
make sure that we have ongoing projects.

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:48.640
But the most important strategic goal of the
chairmanship is to raise awareness of the

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:49.640
Arctic.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:52.870
And not so much for the rest of the world,
because I think the rest of the world gets

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:56.240
it, but to raise awareness within the United
States of America.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:02.910
Because, as I've said many times, we are detached
from our Arctic.

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:08.240
The Alaskans understand it very well, but
the rest of the country really doesn't think

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:10.440
about the United States being an Arctic nation.

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:14.881
And I think through the GLACIER Conference,
the president's significant investment of

00:09:14.881 --> 00:09:19.940
time going to that conference-and not only
taking that time, but I can tell you, I've

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:26.611
spoken to senior officials in the White House
after his visit that said that the president

00:09:26.611 --> 00:09:34.170
said that event was worthy of hyperbolic statements,
and the good news within the White House when

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:37.630
the president came back was that it was now
his number-one priority.

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:43.430
The bad news was it's now his number-one priority,
because anything that we do in the Arctic,

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:47.980
as Senator Murkowski is probably going to
tell you, is new business.

00:09:47.980 --> 00:09:53.620
And when you're in a no-growth budget cycle,
anything that you take on either has to displace

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:58.000
something else in the budget, or you got to
find new resources to pay for it.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:00.900
BORGERSON: That's a perfect segue to Alaska.

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:03.810
We are an Arctic nation because of our largest
state.

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:10.560
Senator Murkowski, share with us, as you might,
sort of Alaskans' worldview on this Arctic

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:15.110
change that's happening, and if you wouldn't
mind also speaking a little bit to your ideas

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:17.800
and your thinking about energy policy in particular.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:24.850
MURKOWSKI: Well, thank you for the opportunity
to be with you, and to those here at CSIS

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:25.850
(sic).

00:10:25.850 --> 00:10:34.140
You know, I get frustrated at times, and I
know that we've had an opportunity to air

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:44.700
some of my frustration, that the rest of the
country doesn't appreciate what the role is

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:52.510
that we play-not Alaska as the state, but
the United States as that Arctic nation.

00:10:52.510 --> 00:10:59.600
And so, just as Admiral Papp has outlined,
and Ambassador Brzezinski, the awareness that

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:08.829
has been created truly within the past year,
18 months I think has been not only recognized,

00:11:08.829 --> 00:11:12.940
but appreciated by Alaskans, who have said,
good, it's about time.

00:11:12.940 --> 00:11:16.589
It's about time everybody started paying attention
to us.

00:11:16.589 --> 00:11:24.450
And some of that frustration has been, well,
why are you just looking northward now?

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:33.820
There have been changes that we have seen
in the Arctic, in the northern parts of the

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:40.430
country, for years now, and everyone is just
now paying attention.

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:42.460
What has taken you so long?

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:48.130
So a couple-a couple points to raise here
this morning.

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:51.889
I'm asked if Alaskans are worried.

00:11:51.889 --> 00:11:53.470
Are they concerned?

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:58.570
Are they-are they paying attention to what
we're seeing with the changing environment

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:01.700
around our state?

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:09.510
And you point out some of the recent analysis
in terms of temperatures and the significance

00:12:09.510 --> 00:12:11.420
of that.

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:17.800
We come from a-from a colder climate, where
it's dark a lot of the year, and we joke about

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:23.790
the fact that, well, you know, I'm OK with
it warming a few degrees because it's been

00:12:23.790 --> 00:12:25.350
pretty darn cold around here.

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:30.980
But people like myself, who appreciate winter
probably more than anything-I'm a skier-I'm

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:40.910
not-I'm not so good with the fact that we
are seeing warmer temperatures in the wintertime

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:47.890
that are-that are changing the way that Alaskans
really operate.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:51.269
You think about things like winter sports.

00:12:51.269 --> 00:13:00.140
The Iditarod is probably the most well-known
of our wintertime sports activities.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:07.320
And our reality is that, at the beginning
of the 1,100-mile race between Anchorage and

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:15.590
Nome, to start things off this year, we were
actually looking to bring snow down from Fairbanks

00:13:15.590 --> 00:13:17.770
by railcar to start the race.

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:25.630
Kind of a crazy notion, but that was the reality
that we were dealing with because our precipitation

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:35.029
levels have been notably altered as we're
seeing the warmer temperatures.

00:13:35.029 --> 00:13:42.730
It's having an impact on our native people
in terms of the subsistence foods that they

00:13:42.730 --> 00:13:44.180
are used to.

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:51.760
You're seeing different-you're seeing different
migration patterns with caribou or moose as

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:55.529
browse is changing.

00:13:55.529 --> 00:14:05.019
We're seeing impact to the fisheries, assumably
because there's changes in water temperature

00:14:05.019 --> 00:14:10.430
that impact the timing of when fish are coming.

00:14:10.430 --> 00:14:15.589
And so there is-there is-there is a disruption
that we are seeing.

00:14:15.589 --> 00:14:16.649
It is real.

00:14:16.649 --> 00:14:19.050
It is tangible.

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:27.990
And so the issue for those who are living
in it is, how do you-how do you adapt to these

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:30.820
changing conditions?

00:14:30.820 --> 00:14:40.190
And when this-you know, when you're in a society
where you've very-almost nomadic and moving

00:14:40.190 --> 00:14:47.610
around and following the animals, following
the fish, you can-you can adjust.

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:53.420
But any more-the communities are anchored
by an airstrip, anchored by a school that

00:14:53.420 --> 00:15:00.650
has been put in place, and so now when then
fish are not there or the game is not there,

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:05.810
you have-you have communities that are struggling
in significant ways.

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:17.430
So the impacts that we're seeing in Alaska
are real, and the extent to which our communities,

00:15:17.430 --> 00:15:24.480
our people are able to adapt is an issue that
we are dealing with.

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:30.339
You threw out where we are with energy policy,
so that's a big jump.

00:15:30.339 --> 00:15:38.670
We are a state that is blessed with abundant
and extraordinary resources, and our challenge

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:46.090
has been how you can access those resources
in a way that is cost-efficient; that is consistent

00:15:46.090 --> 00:15:56.570
with good, strong environmental stewardship;
and that works to alleviate some of the-some

00:15:56.570 --> 00:16:03.769
of the issues that our people face: paying
some of the highest costs for energy in the

00:16:03.769 --> 00:16:04.769
country.

00:16:04.769 --> 00:16:10.680
I've got some interns that are with me today
who were making calls around to various villages

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:19.509
around the state to find out what people are
paying for diesel and gasoline and home heating

00:16:19.509 --> 00:16:20.730
oil.

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:24.130
And I asked, well, what's the highest price
that anybody's paying right now.

00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:25.130
Nine bucks a gallon.

00:16:25.130 --> 00:16:26.130
BORGERSON: Wow.

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:31.230
MURKOWSKI: You know, that's significant when
you are-significant when you're anywhere,

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:39.580
much less in a subsistence economy and resources
are limited.

00:16:39.580 --> 00:16:43.830
BORGERSON: As a U.S. senator, I have to ask,
as the Senate is the one that gives us advice

00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:49.449
and consent to treaties, what are the prospects
for the Law of the Sea Convention, which is

00:16:49.449 --> 00:16:53.180
the treaty that governs the Arctic Ocean,
first?

00:16:53.180 --> 00:16:58.380
And if I-if you might, also, the Senate also
has the nation's checkbook, and icebreakers

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:00.370
require money to build.

00:17:00.370 --> 00:17:07.970
So could you give us maybe quickly where are
we in being one of the last nations with a

00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:10.539
coastline not to join the treaty?

00:17:10.539 --> 00:17:14.410
And where are we in our icebreaker commitments?

00:17:14.410 --> 00:17:21.490
MURKOWSKI: I wish that I could say that we
were closer to ratification of Law of the

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:22.490
Sea Treaty.

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:25.429
This is something that I have long supported.

00:17:25.429 --> 00:17:31.370
When Secretary Kerry was Senator Kerry and
head of the Foreign Relations Committee, we

00:17:31.370 --> 00:17:39.000
were working-I think we had-we'd actually
moved the ball a little bit in gaining the

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:40.799
necessary support.

00:17:40.799 --> 00:17:48.190
It has not been high on the priority list
of the Foreign Relations Committee, unfortunately.

00:17:48.190 --> 00:17:52.470
It is something that I think-and I look to
these people here in this room.

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:57.160
It is something that the Senate needs to be
pushed on.

00:17:57.160 --> 00:17:59.059
You're not going to see it happen this year.

00:17:59.059 --> 00:18:03.190
I can-there's not a lot of things that I'm
willing to place money on, but this one is

00:18:03.190 --> 00:18:05.340
one you're not going to see happen this year.

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:12.190
But I do think that it is something that we
need to get advanced up on the priority list.

00:18:12.190 --> 00:18:13.280
BORGERSON: I agree with you.

00:18:13.280 --> 00:18:14.860
And-
MURKOWSKI: Well, let's work on that, because

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:16.070
it's a big deal.

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:17.970
BORGERSON: All right, yeah.

00:18:17.970 --> 00:18:18.970
And, icebreakers?

00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:19.970
MURKOWSKI: Icebreakers.

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:20.970
Good news.

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:21.970
Good news.

00:18:21.970 --> 00:18:27.240
I'm actually leaving here and going to yet
another Appropriations markup this morning.

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:37.190
But just now 10 days ago we in the Appropriations
Committee were able to advance $1 billion

00:18:37.190 --> 00:18:39.170
for construction of an icebreaker.

00:18:39.170 --> 00:18:45.720
This is something that we have been pushing
for a long, long time.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:50.809
Through the appropriations process we've been
getting, you know, 4 million (dollars) here,

00:18:50.809 --> 00:18:52.230
7 million (dollars) there.

00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:56.420
And you joke and you say, well, that doesn't
even get you a porthole.

00:18:56.420 --> 00:19:03.160
The significance of the president coming to
Alaska last year, and announcing the acceleration

00:19:03.160 --> 00:19:10.789
of construction, and moving towards an icebreaker
fleet-we've got to do more than one in order

00:19:10.789 --> 00:19:14.290
to constitute a fleet, but we're making headway.

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:16.770
And that is significant for us.

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:18.100
BORGERSON: Terrific.

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:19.840
We're about to open up to the floor for questions.

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:23.320
But, Ambassador Brzezinski, we haven't mentioned
Russia yet.

00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:27.600
Russia has a big Arctic coastline, major Arctic
power and player.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:32.580
The issue before last in Foreign Affairs was
dedicated to our relationship with Russia,

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:35.710
which is nuanced and complicated.

00:19:35.710 --> 00:19:39.809
Help us, if you might, understand how Russia
fits into U.S. Arctic policy.

00:19:39.809 --> 00:19:43.120
Maybe, Admiral Papp, you might be able to
add a thought to that.

00:19:43.120 --> 00:19:48.400
And then how do you think about maybe best
practices of other Arctic nations, what they're

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:52.500
getting right in the Arctic, and maybe lessons
learned that we want to pay attention to as

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:56.600
a country, so that we don't make some of the
mistakes that others might have made there.

00:19:56.600 --> 00:20:03.600
BRZEZINSKI: Well, our work with Russia in
the Arctic focuses on science and research,

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:12.360
and we remain proactively collaborative with
the Russians, and are welcoming them in terms

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:15.580
of spearheading specific initiatives within
the Arctic Council.

00:20:15.580 --> 00:20:21.150
For example, there is a Science and Cooperation
Task Force within the Arctic Council, the

00:20:21.150 --> 00:20:28.309
Arctic Council being constituted of the eight
members-eight countries above the Arctic Circle:

00:20:28.309 --> 00:20:31.440
the five Nordic countries, Canada, Russia,
and the United States.

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:38.040
We are spearheading a science and cooperation
agreement with Russia, and I am very-I am

00:20:38.040 --> 00:20:41.870
anticipating success within our chairmanship
on that.

00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:47.419
That is a very good example of a tangible
product for the American people and the people

00:20:47.419 --> 00:20:54.860
of the world from the Arctic Council, a consensus-based
organization, and I hope that we see more

00:20:54.860 --> 00:20:55.860
of it.

00:20:55.860 --> 00:21:00.990
We share the same challenges in the Arctic
with Russia and with other countries in the

00:21:00.990 --> 00:21:01.990
world.

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:06.380
It's adapting to the impacts of climate change
for our communities there, and the link between

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:12.710
that change and our own backyards and the
rest of the world, and the ability of our

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:15.900
communities in the Arctic to adapt to those
changes.

00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:23.520
I actually am hopeful for more of a shared
and-you know, shared learning between the

00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:28.550
countries of the Arctic regarding what we
know about the Arctic, lessons learned, and

00:21:28.550 --> 00:21:29.659
so forth.

00:21:29.659 --> 00:21:35.390
I want to emphasize that, you know, we have
a president who's personally interested in

00:21:35.390 --> 00:21:36.390
the Arctic.

00:21:36.390 --> 00:21:41.549
And since coming back from Alaska, he has
asked for updates on what have we done lately

00:21:41.549 --> 00:21:45.419
in terms of our commitments and promises in
the Arctic.

00:21:45.419 --> 00:21:49.690
And we are-one of the goals of the Arctic
Executive Steering Committee in the White

00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:56.809
House is to drive forward milestones and accomplishments
pertaining to those commitments and promises,

00:21:56.809 --> 00:22:02.360
whether it's EPA water and sanitation grants
in rural Alaska, or USDA high-cost energy

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:04.190
grants, and so forth.

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:14.120
A capstone of the Obama administration's Arctic
approach in 2016 will be that the White House

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:20.380
will convene, for the first time ever, a meeting
of science ministers from around the world

00:22:20.380 --> 00:22:22.040
on the topic of Arctic science.

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:27.110
There's never been a meeting of science ministers
from the countries around the world on what

00:22:27.110 --> 00:22:32.500
is happening in the Arctic, and how we can
develop a collective and joint approach.

00:22:32.500 --> 00:22:37.130
That will occur on September 28th, I'm very
pleased to say.

00:22:37.130 --> 00:22:42.160
The White House is inviting not just the Arctic
countries, but countries that have shown a

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:46.380
significant interest in investing in Arctic
science.

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:53.740
So we're inviting 20-plus countries to this
event to-and it'll be a working ministerial,

00:22:53.740 --> 00:22:55.500
which will include Russia.

00:22:55.500 --> 00:22:59.630
And we very much are seeking proposals and
ideas from these countries.

00:22:59.630 --> 00:23:01.549
It's a very open-minded approach.

00:23:01.549 --> 00:23:06.560
It's not a summit in which we, the Americans,
are simply saying here are our deliverables,

00:23:06.560 --> 00:23:09.549
you foreign countries come bring your money
and join us.

00:23:09.549 --> 00:23:13.010
Instead, it's please bring us your ideas and
your proposals.

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:15.540
And that includes Russia as well.

00:23:15.540 --> 00:23:20.740
And the thesis of that, as it pertains to
Arctic science, is that science is at the

00:23:20.740 --> 00:23:25.200
foundation of the challenges we face there,
whether it's the challenges of subsistence

00:23:25.200 --> 00:23:30.620
communities-energy, water, sanitation-and
a collective approach is the most effective

00:23:30.620 --> 00:23:31.620
approach.

00:23:31.620 --> 00:23:32.620
BORGERSON: Yeah.

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:33.620
Great.

00:23:33.620 --> 00:23:34.620
Admiral Papp, do you want to add a thought
to that?

00:23:34.620 --> 00:23:37.900
PAPP: I would, because this whole issue with
Russia I think is one of the most important

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:40.630
things that we have associated with the Arctic
Council.

00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:45.821
And for those of you who may not be entirely
familiar with the Arctic Council, I refer

00:23:45.821 --> 00:23:49.150
to it sometimes as a coalition of volunteers.

00:23:49.150 --> 00:23:54.559
Obviously, it's a small group, the eight countries
that are within the Arctic region.

00:23:54.559 --> 00:23:57.289
But it's an international forum.

00:23:57.289 --> 00:24:01.559
Everything that's done within the Arctic Council
is done based on the consensus of all eight

00:24:01.559 --> 00:24:02.789
countries.

00:24:02.789 --> 00:24:07.820
So if we want to admit another observer, you
have to have the consensus of all eight countries.

00:24:07.820 --> 00:24:12.529
If you want to get your chairmanship program
approved, you have to have the consensus of

00:24:12.529 --> 00:24:14.090
all eight countries.

00:24:14.090 --> 00:24:19.240
And as Papp comes into the job as special
representative we have just placed sanctions,

00:24:19.240 --> 00:24:23.430
rightly so, on Russia for their aggression
in the Ukraine.

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:27.020
So we don't have a normal situation.

00:24:27.020 --> 00:24:28.140
And there is tension.

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:32.490
But one of the things that Secretary Kerry
and the president have stressed is we need

00:24:32.490 --> 00:24:35.470
the Arctic to be a region of peace and stability.

00:24:35.470 --> 00:24:39.560
The Arctic Council works towards that.

00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:43.980
And as we negotiated our chairmanship program,
perhaps the most important visit I did was

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:46.060
my visit to Moscow.

00:24:46.060 --> 00:24:50.960
And there were literally people in the city
who said I should not go to Moscow.

00:24:50.960 --> 00:24:55.720
But the secretary of state and the president
decided it was important for this program.

00:24:55.720 --> 00:25:02.659
And what I would say is I have met some very
reasoned, sober, professional individuals

00:25:02.659 --> 00:25:08.410
that work the Arctic issues within Russia.

00:25:08.410 --> 00:25:14.440
Their senior Arctic official gave us the most
thorough critique of our chairmanship program

00:25:14.440 --> 00:25:16.250
of any of the other countries.

00:25:16.250 --> 00:25:22.510
Gave us a lot of suggestions for improvement.

00:25:22.510 --> 00:25:26.960
And Vladimir Barbin has been one of our best
partners on the Arctic Council in terms of

00:25:26.960 --> 00:25:30.299
moving our chairmanship program forward.

00:25:30.299 --> 00:25:36.799
So while we rightly hold their feet to the
fire for activities in Ukraine, what I've

00:25:36.799 --> 00:25:43.130
learned-I'm not career diplomat, as everybody
knows-but I'm a quick learner.

00:25:43.130 --> 00:25:49.371
And what I have learned in the State Department
is you can take stands on principle or you

00:25:49.371 --> 00:25:53.299
can do things that re in the best interests
of your country.

00:25:53.299 --> 00:25:55.669
Now, they don't have to be mutually exclusive.

00:25:55.669 --> 00:25:57.200
There are gray areas.

00:25:57.200 --> 00:26:02.450
There are certain things, like the activities
in the Ukraine, where we take a definite stand

00:26:02.450 --> 00:26:03.450
on principle.

00:26:03.450 --> 00:26:05.809
It's a black or white issue.

00:26:05.809 --> 00:26:10.900
And then there are other things, frankly,
we need the Russian cooperation for within

00:26:10.900 --> 00:26:13.690
the world that are in our country's best interests.

00:26:13.690 --> 00:26:16.350
So there are gray areas there.

00:26:16.350 --> 00:26:19.690
The Arctic is one of those areas that is black
or white.

00:26:19.690 --> 00:26:22.730
And we have great cooperation going on there.

00:26:22.730 --> 00:26:28.710
And I think that perceptions of their activities
in the Arctic are a little overblown and prone

00:26:28.710 --> 00:26:29.710
to rhetoric.

00:26:29.710 --> 00:26:31.419
And hopefully we'll get into some of that
in the Q&amp;A.

00:26:31.419 --> 00:26:35.460
BORGERSON: Or increasingly blue, with all
the ocean now that you see instead of ice.

00:26:35.460 --> 00:26:37.030
PAPP: Or blue.

00:26:37.030 --> 00:26:38.070
BORGERSON: Yeah.

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:44.000
Just anecdotally, quickly, as relates to Russian
sort of participation in collaborative efforts

00:26:44.000 --> 00:26:50.289
in diplomacy, I was lucky enough to have the
fun opportunity to co-found the Arctic Circle

00:26:50.289 --> 00:26:55.419
NGO with the president of Iceland and Alice
Rogoff Rubenstein, and a few others.

00:26:55.419 --> 00:26:57.950
And it was a cocktail napkin a few years ago.

00:26:57.950 --> 00:27:03.920
We now fill the Harpa opera hall in Reykjavik
with thousands of people from around the world.

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:05.500
And the spirit of it is a lot of fun.

00:27:05.500 --> 00:27:07.179
I think everybody up here has attended.

00:27:07.179 --> 00:27:13.940
And Russia every year, including the Chinese,
and Singapore, and South Koreans, and Japanese,

00:27:13.940 --> 00:27:19.830
and surprising people you might not expect,
send big delegations, led usually by Ambassador

00:27:19.830 --> 00:27:21.190
Chilingarov.

00:27:21.190 --> 00:27:24.720
And the spirit of the interaction with them
has really been quite lovely.

00:27:24.720 --> 00:27:26.990
And you all are invited this October.

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:27.990
ArcticCircle.org.

00:27:27.990 --> 00:27:31.490
It's a great party focused on the Arctic in
Reykjavik.

00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:32.780
So we'll shift to the floor now.

00:27:32.780 --> 00:27:36.960
If we could start with Esther, please, and
I believe there's a microphone that'll roam.

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:37.960
Thank you.

00:27:37.960 --> 00:27:38.960
Q: Good morning.

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:42.570
First, I'd like to thank the distinguished
panel for raising this issue, and for all

00:27:42.570 --> 00:27:44.110
of us for being here and those online.

00:27:44.110 --> 00:27:45.580
This is so important now.

00:27:45.580 --> 00:27:46.580
My name is Esther Brimmer.

00:27:46.580 --> 00:27:49.270
I have the honor of being the project director
for the Task Force on the Arctic.

00:27:49.270 --> 00:27:52.809
And so very much welcome this session and
the expertise and the interest of all those

00:27:52.809 --> 00:27:53.809
in the room.

00:27:53.809 --> 00:27:56.760
I'm also formerly of the State Department
as assistant secretary for international organization,

00:27:56.760 --> 00:27:59.310
so I will ask two diplomatic questions.

00:27:59.310 --> 00:28:04.360
The first is, to ask about another important
Arctic partner, Canada, our close neighbor

00:28:04.360 --> 00:28:05.360
and ally.

00:28:05.360 --> 00:28:09.299
And indeed, we've had a long-standing issue
about how we think about the northwest passage

00:28:09.299 --> 00:28:11.179
or the Arctic Archipelago.

00:28:11.179 --> 00:28:15.700
What would you see as the next steps to addressing
this important issue with our close neighbor?

00:28:15.700 --> 00:28:17.510
And secondly, a question about the Arctic
Council.

00:28:17.510 --> 00:28:21.410
Of course, the Arctic Council has benefited
from the consensus environment.

00:28:21.410 --> 00:28:25.400
It's a very special organization, perhaps
unique among our international organizations.

00:28:25.400 --> 00:28:27.179
Yet there are issues that are not handled
there.

00:28:27.179 --> 00:28:32.260
Of course, traditional security issues are
not raised in this forum, for very clear reasons.

00:28:32.260 --> 00:28:36.779
However, are there other places, other fora
in which you think these issues should be

00:28:36.779 --> 00:28:37.779
raised?

00:28:37.779 --> 00:28:38.779
Thank you.

00:28:38.779 --> 00:28:39.779
BORGERSON: Great.

00:28:39.779 --> 00:28:40.779
Thank you.

00:28:40.779 --> 00:28:42.110
Ambassador Brzezinski, would you speak to-because
we were just talking about, actually, Canada

00:28:42.110 --> 00:28:43.110
and the northwest passage.

00:28:43.110 --> 00:28:45.360
Admiral Papp, would you pick up the Arctic
Council, please?

00:28:45.360 --> 00:28:46.360
BRZEZINSKI: Thank you.

00:28:46.360 --> 00:28:48.510
Thank you, Esther, for that question.

00:28:48.510 --> 00:28:56.720
With the election of Prime Minister Justin
Trudeau, we explored making the Arctic a place

00:28:56.720 --> 00:29:01.730
where both countries could advance a joint
leadership model, as we headed towards the

00:29:01.730 --> 00:29:04.830
Justin Trudeau state visit in March.

00:29:04.830 --> 00:29:12.220
And we were incredibly pleased to see that
the Privy Council in Canada absolutely embraced

00:29:12.220 --> 00:29:21.200
an ascension of the U.S.-Canadian relationship,
really, looking back historically, more than

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:27.610
at any time since Mackenzie King was prime
minister during World War II, around the Arctic

00:29:27.610 --> 00:29:31.700
issue, around energy, around the environment.

00:29:31.700 --> 00:29:39.350
And an extremely substantial joint statement
was released that was subsequently joined

00:29:39.350 --> 00:29:48.310
by the five Nordic leaders during their state
visit in May, which commits six of our Arctic

00:29:48.310 --> 00:29:55.149
neighbors, and ourselves, so seven of the
eight Arctic countries, to a principled approach

00:29:55.149 --> 00:29:59.770
on commercial activity in the Arctic in the
future to ensure that it's consistent with

00:29:59.770 --> 00:30:03.160
environmental sustainability criteria.

00:30:03.160 --> 00:30:05.740
That is an important development.

00:30:05.740 --> 00:30:13.190
And the starting point, the anchor for that,
was the Canadian government's full embrace

00:30:13.190 --> 00:30:14.190
on the Arctic.

00:30:14.190 --> 00:30:21.179
So I feel-so I thank you for asking that,
because, Scott, you raised the question of

00:30:21.179 --> 00:30:23.909
working with Russia, which is one opportunity.

00:30:23.909 --> 00:30:32.029
A significant additional opportunity is working
through our very special neighborhood relationship

00:30:32.029 --> 00:30:34.920
that we have with Canada on the Arctic.

00:30:34.920 --> 00:30:38.390
And that's what we're doing to do as we run
through the tape in the-towards the end of

00:30:38.390 --> 00:30:39.390
this administration.

00:30:39.390 --> 00:30:41.640
BORGERSON: And the new Canadian prime minister
just visited Washington, D.C.

00:30:41.640 --> 00:30:42.640
BRZEZINSKI: That's right.

00:30:42.640 --> 00:30:45.341
BORGERSON: And was Arctic part of the agenda
with the discussion with the president?

00:30:45.341 --> 00:30:46.429
BRZEZINSKI: The Arctic was part of the agenda.

00:30:46.429 --> 00:30:49.059
It was part of the discussion with the president.

00:30:49.059 --> 00:30:54.789
And a joint statement was released by both
countries, committing both to a new leadership

00:30:54.789 --> 00:31:03.860
model, zeroing in on milestones pertaining
to energy, the environment in the Arctic.

00:31:03.860 --> 00:31:09.010
And now we are seeking-because it's one thing
to announce these milestones, another thing

00:31:09.010 --> 00:31:12.390
to produce accountability to achieve them.

00:31:12.390 --> 00:31:14.720
And that's one the thing the president has
been very clear.

00:31:14.720 --> 00:31:16.240
We're not just going to announce principles.

00:31:16.240 --> 00:31:19.490
We're going to march forward and achieve milestones.

00:31:19.490 --> 00:31:21.360
And so that's what we're zeroing in on right
now.

00:31:21.360 --> 00:31:22.360
BORGERSON: Great.

00:31:22.360 --> 00:31:24.150
Admiral Papp, the Arctic Council question?

00:31:24.150 --> 00:31:30.460
PAPP: Yes, we get plenty of suggestions on
how we might change the Arctic Council coming

00:31:30.460 --> 00:31:32.690
from many of the think tanks here in Washington,
D.C.

00:31:32.690 --> 00:31:39.039
And most frequently the issue that's suggested
to us is that we should bring in-and let me

00:31:39.039 --> 00:31:43.120
clarify terms, lest there is anybody that
doesn't understand.

00:31:43.120 --> 00:31:48.730
When we talk security, everybody's mind goes
to what I call national defense.

00:31:48.730 --> 00:31:55.940
So we look at the broader issues of security-economic,
environment, energy, food, et cetera.

00:31:55.940 --> 00:32:02.029
And national defense-if you read the national
security strategy of the United States-national

00:32:02.029 --> 00:32:03.940
defense is a portion of that.

00:32:03.940 --> 00:32:06.100
People want to read defense into it.

00:32:06.100 --> 00:32:11.309
And what I would say is, in my opinion, that's
the quickest way to break down the Arctic

00:32:11.309 --> 00:32:16.770
Council, because there are other venues that
can handle that.

00:32:16.770 --> 00:32:21.960
Now, we look at NATO as being a treaty for
collective self-defense.

00:32:21.960 --> 00:32:25.610
I think you look at it differently when you
look at it from Moscow.

00:32:25.610 --> 00:32:32.169
You look at it from Moscow, and culturally
they look at it as being ganged-up against.

00:32:32.169 --> 00:32:40.490
If the European Union collectively is good
for economic issues, and for the collected

00:32:40.490 --> 00:32:47.120
prosperity of Europe, but if you look at it
from Moscow it certainly appears to be economically

00:32:47.120 --> 00:32:48.289
ganged-up against them.

00:32:48.289 --> 00:32:52.549
They have that sort of mindset, at least as
the way I read it.

00:32:52.549 --> 00:32:57.140
So if you start bringing those things into
the Arctic Council, you start breaking consensus

00:32:57.140 --> 00:33:00.490
or giving you the potential for losing consensus.

00:33:00.490 --> 00:33:06.900
And I would say that when you have a country
that has about half the coastline in the Arctic,

00:33:06.900 --> 00:33:17.840
has probably legitimate claims to huge extended
continental shelf, that you need to take their

00:33:17.840 --> 00:33:20.570
concerns into consideration when you do it.

00:33:20.570 --> 00:33:25.870
And I think the more you bring defense and
other things into-try to bring them into the

00:33:25.870 --> 00:33:32.169
Arctic Council, you start losing that consensus
that is so vitally important to keeping the

00:33:32.169 --> 00:33:34.470
Arctic a place of peace and stability.

00:33:34.470 --> 00:33:39.029
BORGERSON: I should have mentioned this, Professor
Brimmer, to please mention your name and your

00:33:39.029 --> 00:33:41.899
affiliation when you ask your question, if
you could.

00:33:41.899 --> 00:33:42.899
Rob.

00:33:42.899 --> 00:33:46.100
Q: Rob Quartel, NTELEX.

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:48.830
I'm sure others have the same question in
their mind.

00:33:48.830 --> 00:33:52.250
If the ice is shrinking, why do we need icebreakers?

00:33:52.250 --> 00:33:57.270
And I would appreciate you putting that in
an economic and strategic context, perspective.

00:33:57.270 --> 00:33:58.590
BORGERSON: OK.

00:33:58.590 --> 00:34:02.010
I think the former commandant of the Coast
Guard would be perfect to answer that question.

00:34:02.010 --> 00:34:03.010
(Laughter.)

00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:04.010
PAPP: Yeah.

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:05.010
Well, clearly the ice is shrinking.

00:34:05.010 --> 00:34:11.990
But, I mean, I've spent a lot of time talking
to scientists, and my own personal observations.

00:34:11.990 --> 00:34:18.590
And I would say that probably for the next
50 years or so, at least, which I think is

00:34:18.590 --> 00:34:24.079
fair to project out on and is general the
service life of a ship, there's going to be

00:34:24.079 --> 00:34:31.410
increased need for ice breakers, because you
have an increase in maritime traffic around

00:34:31.410 --> 00:34:34.909
the Arctic and, potentially by midcentury,
through the Arctic.

00:34:34.909 --> 00:34:38.589
There's going to continue to be winter sea
ice.

00:34:38.589 --> 00:34:46.359
But people, entrepreneurs, are going to continually
stretch and test and push the limits on how-because

00:34:46.359 --> 00:34:51.520
there is a significant economic advantage
to these northern sea routes.

00:34:51.520 --> 00:34:52.970
That's why China is up there.

00:34:52.970 --> 00:34:57.810
This is something that's been doing on for
500 years, people looking for shorter sea

00:34:57.810 --> 00:34:58.810
routes.

00:34:58.810 --> 00:35:04.410
So in order for countries to live up to their
responsibilities for safety and security,

00:35:04.410 --> 00:35:09.530
assured access to a region that they're responsible
for, and to support the people who live within

00:35:09.530 --> 00:35:11.620
the region, you have to have ice breakers.

00:35:11.620 --> 00:35:13.160
The ice is going to be there.

00:35:13.160 --> 00:35:19.120
And because it breaks up quicker, there's
movement, the ice is actually more dangerous

00:35:19.120 --> 00:35:24.650
now, because there's a lot more movement of
large flows of ice that you need icebreakers

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:25.770
up there to deal with.

00:35:25.770 --> 00:35:27.119
BORGERSON: That felt like a softball, Rob.

00:35:27.119 --> 00:35:28.210
He hit that one.

00:35:28.210 --> 00:35:29.210
(Laughter.)

00:35:29.210 --> 00:35:30.210
Ambassador Brzezinski?

00:35:30.210 --> 00:35:33.040
BRZEZINSKI: In Washington, you are your budget.

00:35:33.040 --> 00:35:38.240
And I'm proud that in February the president
rolled out a budget proposal that really,

00:35:38.240 --> 00:35:42.750
for the first time, shows a national imperative
on the Arctic, through funding what it will

00:35:42.750 --> 00:35:49.680
take to build an icebreaker-and it was plural,
icebreakers, when the president made the announcement

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:53.869
in September in Alaska, and breaking the logjam
there.

00:35:53.869 --> 00:36:00.440
A national imperative pertaining to resilience
and coastal erosion and adaptation, $2 billion.

00:36:00.440 --> 00:36:04.750
A national imperative as it pertains to science-Arctic
science.

00:36:04.750 --> 00:36:12.010
But I want to emphasize that the Arctic is
of a scale and of an expense kind of unseen

00:36:12.010 --> 00:36:15.970
elsewhere in the world in terms of a place
to operate.

00:36:15.970 --> 00:36:23.320
And there are economic and financial benefits
that can be reported out to the American people

00:36:23.320 --> 00:36:29.040
by working collectively with countries around
this world on this challenge, as opposed to

00:36:29.040 --> 00:36:31.560
just working with ourselves.

00:36:31.560 --> 00:36:36.780
That's one of the goals of the White House
Arctic Science Ministerial in late September,

00:36:36.780 --> 00:36:44.089
to develop alignments with governments around
the world in a way that captures a range of

00:36:44.089 --> 00:36:50.370
engagement and a range of expense that can
produce economic efficiencies reportable to

00:36:50.370 --> 00:36:51.640
our people, we hope.

00:36:51.640 --> 00:36:54.050
PAPP: I would also add something here.

00:36:54.050 --> 00:36:58.609
You know, we're focused on the Arctic today,
but the United States has responsibilities

00:36:58.609 --> 00:37:00.260
in Antarctica as well.

00:37:00.260 --> 00:37:05.250
We have a scientific mission there that requires
a break in to resupply each year which, once

00:37:05.250 --> 00:37:06.270
again, requires ice breakers.

00:37:06.270 --> 00:37:10.740
MURKOWSKI: I was just going to add exactly
that.

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:19.250
We're talking Arctic here, that's the focus,
but we have one polar strength icebreaker

00:37:19.250 --> 00:37:23.099
that's working in McMurdo now.

00:37:23.099 --> 00:37:26.950
The useful life, she's been rehabbed once.

00:37:26.950 --> 00:37:34.820
There's probably four to six, maybe stretch
it to seven years left in the Polar Star.

00:37:34.820 --> 00:37:39.349
The Polar Sea is sitting at dock in Seattle.

00:37:39.349 --> 00:37:41.480
She's been cannibalized for parts.

00:37:41.480 --> 00:37:43.570
I don't think you ever see her back in the
water.

00:37:43.570 --> 00:37:45.960
There's an effort to try to put her back in
too.

00:37:45.960 --> 00:37:49.810
But our reality is that's what we have.

00:37:49.810 --> 00:37:55.380
You'll never see-I don't think you'll ever
see the Polar Star in the Arctic again because

00:37:55.380 --> 00:38:01.530
of the commitment to do the breakout in Antarctica
going forward.

00:38:01.530 --> 00:38:08.170
You're going to have a period of eight to
10 years to build a new icebreaker.

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:09.170
Do the math there.

00:38:09.170 --> 00:38:11.329
you got a gap, folks.

00:38:11.329 --> 00:38:17.760
So when you're talking about an icebreaker
fleet for the United States, you need to recognize

00:38:17.760 --> 00:38:24.660
what it is that we're already committed to
cover and what it is we need to begin to cover,

00:38:24.660 --> 00:38:27.330
in a way that is far more aggressive than
we've ever seen.

00:38:27.330 --> 00:38:28.330
BORGERSON: Thank you.

00:38:28.330 --> 00:38:30.850
For room diversity, let's go to the back,
if we could.

00:38:30.850 --> 00:38:31.850
Sir.

00:38:31.850 --> 00:38:35.040
We'll come back up front afterwards, I promise.

00:38:35.040 --> 00:38:36.320
Q: Thanks.

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:38.250
My name is Marc Grossman of The Cohen Group.

00:38:38.250 --> 00:38:40.690
First of all, thank you very much for keeping
this on the agenda.

00:38:40.690 --> 00:38:42.250
It's a really important thing.

00:38:42.250 --> 00:38:44.500
And also, for the broad definition of security.

00:38:44.500 --> 00:38:46.150
I think that's also extremely important.

00:38:46.150 --> 00:38:47.420
I have two questions.

00:38:47.420 --> 00:38:53.700
One is given the broad definition of security-native
peoples, food, agriculture, water, health,

00:38:53.700 --> 00:38:58.530
safety, energy-how is it that you all are
making sure that decisions that are being

00:38:58.530 --> 00:39:04.140
kept today in places like EPA, that you talked
about, Mark, or the Commerce Department, or

00:39:04.140 --> 00:39:09.220
the Agriculture Department, or the Interior
Department, have this national security dimension

00:39:09.220 --> 00:39:10.660
connected to it?

00:39:10.660 --> 00:39:14.580
And secondly, I was interested that so far
really not very much conversation about the

00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:16.180
role of the private sector.

00:39:16.180 --> 00:39:19.460
I know, Admiral Papp, you've talked about
the need for ports up there in the Arctic,

00:39:19.460 --> 00:39:20.849
and Senator Murkowski and others.

00:39:20.849 --> 00:39:24.820
So I'd be interested, one, on how to make
sure that people who deciding things today

00:39:24.820 --> 00:39:27.810
do so with national security on their minds.

00:39:27.810 --> 00:39:30.550
And secondly, if you see any role in any of
this for the private sector.

00:39:30.550 --> 00:39:31.550
Thank you very much.

00:39:31.550 --> 00:39:33.440
BORGERSON: Maybe the first part, Ambassador
Brzezinski.

00:39:33.440 --> 00:39:37.240
How from the White House and the executive
branch are you attempting to coordinate that

00:39:37.240 --> 00:39:38.240
very issue?

00:39:38.240 --> 00:39:40.510
BRZEZINSKI: Thank you for that question, Marc.

00:39:40.510 --> 00:39:47.589
The president created a brand-new government
platform at the beginning of 2015 to create

00:39:47.589 --> 00:39:52.470
a unity of purpose and a shared sense of the
challenge and a shared sense of the opportunity

00:39:52.470 --> 00:39:54.810
on the Arctic issue out of the White House.

00:39:54.810 --> 00:39:58.770
That's the Arctic Executive Steering Committee,
which I am the executive director of and John

00:39:58.770 --> 00:40:03.330
Holdren, the president's science advisor,
is the chairman of.

00:40:03.330 --> 00:40:07.150
As you know, Marc, the Arctic Executive Steering
Committee could have been pushed under the

00:40:07.150 --> 00:40:11.560
National Security Council given the domain
awareness and security implications of the

00:40:11.560 --> 00:40:12.690
Arctic issue.

00:40:12.690 --> 00:40:15.800
It could have been placed under the National
Economic Council in the White House given

00:40:15.800 --> 00:40:19.700
the resource development and commercial opportunities
pertaining to the Arctic.

00:40:19.700 --> 00:40:24.569
But it was placed under the science advisor
because of the essential foundation of science

00:40:24.569 --> 00:40:26.380
to all these questions.

00:40:26.380 --> 00:40:34.079
But its membership consists of 25 agencies
and departments of government at the deputy

00:40:34.079 --> 00:40:40.910
secretary level to really prioritize among
all the many things that we could be doing

00:40:40.910 --> 00:40:41.960
on the Arctic.

00:40:41.960 --> 00:40:48.140
And we've reflected on them, what will be
the main ones, and how do they sink under

00:40:48.140 --> 00:40:54.210
our national strategy for the Arctic region,
which President Obama rolled out in 2013.

00:40:54.210 --> 00:41:00.810
We just rolled out an implementation plan
in March of this year, which captures how

00:41:00.810 --> 00:41:07.160
we are trying to integrate all these various
approaches under this national security strategy

00:41:07.160 --> 00:41:08.640
that we have for the Arctic.

00:41:08.640 --> 00:41:13.010
So we have a process specifically designed
to do this.

00:41:13.010 --> 00:41:16.870
And the Arctic Executive Steering Committee's
membership includes agencies like the State

00:41:16.870 --> 00:41:21.180
Department, and the Defense Department, and
the EPA, and the Department of Interior.

00:41:21.180 --> 00:41:27.680
It's really one of those unique government
platforms that is part domestic and part international.

00:41:27.680 --> 00:41:31.460
And we are making sure that the departments
get out to Alaska.

00:41:31.460 --> 00:41:36.010
President Obama's visit to Alaska and the
Arctic last September was not meant to be

00:41:36.010 --> 00:41:40.050
the apex of Washington's high-level engagement
on the Arctic issue.

00:41:40.050 --> 00:41:41.900
It was meant to be the beginning.

00:41:41.900 --> 00:41:47.311
Since then we've seen Transportation Secretary
Foxx, Secretary of Defense Carter, Secretary

00:41:47.311 --> 00:41:51.950
of Treasury Jack Lew, Secretary of Energy
Moniz.

00:41:51.950 --> 00:41:57.210
And Moniz went up to Oscarville and Bethel
in Alaska to bear witness to rural energy

00:41:57.210 --> 00:41:59.540
needs and innovation there.

00:41:59.540 --> 00:42:03.089
Secretary Foxx held a roundtable on native
transportation issues.

00:42:03.089 --> 00:42:08.590
We are making sure that our Cabinet in its
entirety engages on this issue.

00:42:08.590 --> 00:42:14.520
And I believe by the one-year anniversary
of the president's visit in September-this

00:42:14.520 --> 00:42:23.440
coming September, we will have had, if everything
keeps on track, nine of the 18 Cabinet secretaries

00:42:23.440 --> 00:42:26.100
or their deputies in Alaska or the Arctic.

00:42:26.100 --> 00:42:38.250
I know that secretary of state will be going
to the Arctic, looking to the future, and

00:42:38.250 --> 00:42:39.440
others as well.

00:42:39.440 --> 00:42:40.829
And we're continuing to drive that.

00:42:40.829 --> 00:42:41.829
BORGERSON: Senator Murkowski.

00:42:41.829 --> 00:42:45.150
MURKOWSKI: Can I just add the Alaska perspective
to this?

00:42:45.150 --> 00:42:51.520
Because as important as it is to have visits
from the president, from Cabinet members,

00:42:51.520 --> 00:42:58.089
we don't want to be viewed as people that
are in this interesting snow globe.

00:42:58.089 --> 00:43:06.620
We are living, breathing, working people that
have inhabited this area-the indigenous peoples

00:43:06.620 --> 00:43:09.980
have been there for tens of thousands of years.

00:43:09.980 --> 00:43:11.400
And so use us.

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:13.030
We are your experts.

00:43:13.030 --> 00:43:20.460
And it's been said, and I repeat it quite
often, we're not the scientists that have

00:43:20.460 --> 00:43:27.619
the Ph.D.s from all of these academic institutions
around the country and around the globe, but

00:43:27.619 --> 00:43:30.880
we've got Ph.D.s in living.

00:43:30.880 --> 00:43:40.310
The native people who live and work and raise
their family there, the Eskimo whaling commission

00:43:40.310 --> 00:43:47.800
group that I met with yesterday, their life
depends on understanding ice-understanding

00:43:47.800 --> 00:43:56.390
ice, reading the colors, looking at the consistency,
because if they get it wrong they die.

00:43:56.390 --> 00:44:03.920
And so use us-when I say us, I mean the Alaskan
expertise.

00:44:03.920 --> 00:44:06.579
Rely on traditional knowledge.

00:44:06.579 --> 00:44:10.750
Allow consultation to be more than a check-the-box
exercise.

00:44:10.750 --> 00:44:20.430
It is truly something where you sit at the
table with those who, again, are living and

00:44:20.430 --> 00:44:25.020
subsisting in a world that is very, very different
than what we appreciate here in Washington,

00:44:25.020 --> 00:44:26.020
D.C.

00:44:26.020 --> 00:44:34.550
So part of my task, to your question, is to
make sure that the indigenous peoples are

00:44:34.550 --> 00:44:39.200
truly part of this broader national security
focus.

00:44:39.200 --> 00:44:46.800
And I really appreciate what Ambassador Brzezinski
has included in his broader definition, because

00:44:46.800 --> 00:44:52.720
for a long time now we've been pushing to
make sure that the human component of the

00:44:52.720 --> 00:44:55.970
Arctic is taken into account.

00:44:55.970 --> 00:45:01.410
And so making sure that that is there, front
and center, has been a real priority.

00:45:01.410 --> 00:45:05.079
BRZEZINSKI: What the Senator says is absolutely
correct.

00:45:05.079 --> 00:45:10.750
For too long there has been somewhat of a
gap between Washington and Alaska, in my humble

00:45:10.750 --> 00:45:11.750
opinion.

00:45:11.750 --> 00:45:15.690
And we have really leaned forward to proactively
address that.

00:45:15.690 --> 00:45:19.930
Take, for example, the forthcoming White House
Arctic Science Ministerial.

00:45:19.930 --> 00:45:25.701
That will be preceded by a meeting between
the president's science advisors and Alaskan

00:45:25.701 --> 00:45:31.670
native leaders to specifically listen and
learn about traditional knowledge and local

00:45:31.670 --> 00:45:32.670
knowledge.

00:45:32.670 --> 00:45:39.290
Because who better to-(inaudible)-flora and
fauna in Alaska's Arctic than the people who

00:45:39.290 --> 00:45:41.550
have lived there for thousands of years?

00:45:41.550 --> 00:45:44.640
And so the engagement piece is absolutely
critical.

00:45:44.640 --> 00:45:48.361
And when it comes the Arctic Executive Steering
Committee, it is important to note that we

00:45:48.361 --> 00:45:54.470
have Alaskans who are part of that internal
conversation as well, whether it's Fran Ulmer,

00:45:54.470 --> 00:46:00.810
former lieutenant governor, chair of the Arctic
Research-the U.S. Arctic Research Commission,

00:46:00.810 --> 00:46:06.440
whether it's Tommy Beaudreau from the Department
of Interior, Beth Kerttula, lots of others.

00:46:06.440 --> 00:46:11.960
And so bridging that gap through proactive
engagement is absolutely what we have sought

00:46:11.960 --> 00:46:12.960
to do.

00:46:12.960 --> 00:46:16.040
And I was really pleased when Governor Walker
came to the White House for the National Governors

00:46:16.040 --> 00:46:21.990
Association meeting with the president, and
he reflected on this real uptick engagement,

00:46:21.990 --> 00:46:28.180
because who else better to learn from than
the people who see it first-hand every day,

00:46:28.180 --> 00:46:32.250
and who have inherited that from their families
bearing witness over the centuries as well?

00:46:32.250 --> 00:46:36.619
BORGERSON: And could I ask for a brief answer
to the private sector investment question?

00:46:36.619 --> 00:46:39.510
Ports was mentioned but there's lots of other
things happening as well.

00:46:39.510 --> 00:46:47.280
PAPP: I think it's vitally important that
we keep private business concerns at the forefront.

00:46:47.280 --> 00:46:51.900
And in fact, I think the flagship effort of
the Canadian chairmanship was to create the

00:46:51.900 --> 00:46:55.170
Arctic Economic Council, the AEC.

00:46:55.170 --> 00:47:01.790
The AEC has stood up its secretariat at Troms�,
adjacent to the secretariat for the Arctic

00:47:01.790 --> 00:47:03.400
Council.

00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:09.310
And they will bring their expertise and their
interests to help us with many of the projects

00:47:09.310 --> 00:47:12.980
that we have within the Arctic Council.

00:47:12.980 --> 00:47:19.391
But what I would say is that we are-we are
generally in what I would, as a sailor, call

00:47:19.391 --> 00:47:24.401
a stern chase, which means you're constantly
trying to overtake the entrepreneurs who are

00:47:24.401 --> 00:47:25.809
up there.

00:47:25.809 --> 00:47:29.809
Business people aren't going to wait for some
of the things that we're doing within our

00:47:29.809 --> 00:47:31.309
Arctic Council business.

00:47:31.309 --> 00:47:35.440
We have the Crystal Serenity that's going
to take-a cruise ship that's going to have

00:47:35.440 --> 00:47:40.030
2,000 people on board going through the northwest
passage here within another month or so.

00:47:40.030 --> 00:47:47.140
We have-we've had a slowdown in terms of energy
exploration and exploitation, but that's because

00:47:47.140 --> 00:47:48.140
of the oil prices.

00:47:48.140 --> 00:47:52.760
I suspect once they go up again people will
start looking up there again.

00:47:52.760 --> 00:48:01.829
So we in government have an obligation to
be a little foresighted, to look at the impact

00:48:01.829 --> 00:48:06.050
on safety and security, and make sure that
we're prepared for those activities as they

00:48:06.050 --> 00:48:07.050
occur.

00:48:07.050 --> 00:48:09.190
And I think people in industry can help us
in that regard.

00:48:09.190 --> 00:48:10.190
BORGERSON: Yeah.

00:48:10.190 --> 00:48:11.190
MURKOWSKI: Can I just follow on that?

00:48:11.190 --> 00:48:12.190
BORGERSON: Sure.

00:48:12.190 --> 00:48:16.910
MURKOWSKI: Because this is a really important
issue for us up north, is recognizing the

00:48:16.910 --> 00:48:20.330
dearth of infrastructure.

00:48:20.330 --> 00:48:21.770
Ports is very specific.

00:48:21.770 --> 00:48:32.160
When you recognize that effectively Dutch
Harbor is your closest real port to the Arctic.

00:48:32.160 --> 00:48:38.849
It's some 1,100 miles to get from there up
to Barrow area.

00:48:38.849 --> 00:48:47.820
When Shell pulled out last year, I'll tell
you, the emphasis, the push to get a deep

00:48:47.820 --> 00:48:52.240
water port fell to the wayside.

00:48:52.240 --> 00:48:58.710
Wrong, because the Arctic and the need for
infrastructure is not dependent on whether

00:48:58.710 --> 00:49:03.609
or not there's exploration in the Beaufort
or the Chukchi.

00:49:03.609 --> 00:49:12.190
The Arctic is-the U.S. Arctic is an area,
as Admiral says, is expanding in different

00:49:12.190 --> 00:49:17.240
ways, whether it's tourism opportunities,
whether it's just the commercial transit coming

00:49:17.240 --> 00:49:23.050
off the Russian side or coming around the
top.

00:49:23.050 --> 00:49:27.530
There is commercial activity with or without
exploration.

00:49:27.530 --> 00:49:34.150
So making sure that we do not back down on
the imperative to move forward with not only

00:49:34.150 --> 00:49:43.010
one port but a system of ports, recognizing
that communications is an area that we must

00:49:43.010 --> 00:49:45.510
proceed with.

00:49:45.510 --> 00:49:52.329
When Shell was up there, they had put together
really a framework for communications that

00:49:52.329 --> 00:49:54.810
was first of its kind up there.

00:49:54.810 --> 00:49:56.849
Now they're gone.

00:49:56.849 --> 00:50:02.410
Yesterday I had a sit-down with many of the
leadership from up north.

00:50:02.410 --> 00:50:09.430
They're saying: We now need to build this
communications network that Shell had built,

00:50:09.430 --> 00:50:11.900
that now they're gone, and how do we build
this?

00:50:11.900 --> 00:50:14.119
So we're talking about how we do that.

00:50:14.119 --> 00:50:21.150
And where do the resources come from in which
to advance these projects?

00:50:21.150 --> 00:50:26.100
So public-private partnerships has got to
be key in advancing this.

00:50:26.100 --> 00:50:27.410
BORGERSON: Yeah, thank you.

00:50:27.410 --> 00:50:30.770
The Council on Foreign Relations, we pride
ourselves on punctuality.

00:50:30.770 --> 00:50:33.910
And I see it's 9:27; we have three minutes
left of this meeting.

00:50:33.910 --> 00:50:41.150
So we'll take one last question here, if you
could please make it sort of sharp and brief.

00:50:41.150 --> 00:50:43.369
Yeah, microphone on its way.

00:50:43.369 --> 00:50:44.760
One second, the microphone's coming.

00:50:44.760 --> 00:50:45.760
Yeah.

00:50:45.760 --> 00:50:52.680
Q: I'm Rafe Pomerance, chair of Arctic 21,
a network of NGOs focused on the unraveling

00:50:52.680 --> 00:50:55.690
of the Arctic from climate change.

00:50:55.690 --> 00:50:59.579
That unraveling not only impacts the Arctic,
but the globe.

00:50:59.579 --> 00:51:05.369
And my short question, which is-I'll put it
in the form of a comment.

00:51:05.369 --> 00:51:09.890
I think we're missing a point here, which
is, what's the big future issue, or one of

00:51:09.890 --> 00:51:10.890
them?

00:51:10.890 --> 00:51:13.640
And that issue is, what's the Arctic we have
to have?

00:51:13.640 --> 00:51:14.640
That's the question.

00:51:14.640 --> 00:51:18.869
The Arctic has three major pillars of the
climate system, at least.

00:51:18.869 --> 00:51:22.680
Stores massive carbon in permafrost, which
is now thawed.

00:51:22.680 --> 00:51:28.359
It reflects sunlight from sea ice and snow
cover, which is diminishing at an unprecedented

00:51:28.359 --> 00:51:29.470
pace.

00:51:29.470 --> 00:51:33.920
And it holds the fate of Miami and Greenland
in the Arctic eight glaciers from sea level

00:51:33.920 --> 00:51:34.920
rise.

00:51:34.920 --> 00:51:41.080
So the question really is that the fundamental
national strategic issue may be what's the

00:51:41.080 --> 00:51:43.730
Arctic we have to have in the longer run?

00:51:43.730 --> 00:51:44.730
BORGERSON: Yeah.

00:51:44.730 --> 00:51:46.170
In one minute or less.

00:51:46.170 --> 00:51:47.460
(Laughter.)

00:51:47.460 --> 00:51:52.230
If we could, let's just perhaps go down the
panel and have brief concluding thoughts leaping

00:51:52.230 --> 00:51:55.900
off that, and spirt of really sustainability,
which we haven't talked about in his meeting

00:51:55.900 --> 00:51:59.950
as well, of how to balance sort of national
security with that kind of development and

00:51:59.950 --> 00:52:00.950
the environment?

00:52:00.950 --> 00:52:01.950
Admiral Papp.

00:52:01.950 --> 00:52:04.089
PAPP: Rafe, it's good to have you here.

00:52:04.089 --> 00:52:07.470
And he always serves as a conscious when we
come in here.

00:52:07.470 --> 00:52:10.360
And he brings up a very important point.

00:52:10.360 --> 00:52:15.490
When I came into the job and I wanted to generate
interest, and when we started talking about

00:52:15.490 --> 00:52:20.080
the need to raise awareness, I said, well,
what we need is a national imperative.

00:52:20.080 --> 00:52:26.500
And as I looked through the history of Alaska,
any major event or major investment has been

00:52:26.500 --> 00:52:37.000
focused on national defense issues, or perceived
crises, or something like the Valdez oil spill.

00:52:37.000 --> 00:52:41.540
And I said, what is the national imperative
today?

00:52:41.540 --> 00:52:45.561
And actually, it fell right into my hands
because the president announced it.

00:52:45.561 --> 00:52:47.880
It's climate change.

00:52:47.880 --> 00:52:50.599
And he has given that his focus.

00:52:50.599 --> 00:52:56.450
Alaska and the glacier conference and our
Arctic Council chairmanship has served to

00:52:56.450 --> 00:53:01.900
raise awareness for the greater strategic
issue of what's happening with climate change.

00:53:01.900 --> 00:53:07.369
I have to tell you, the more I learn the more
scared I get.

00:53:07.369 --> 00:53:12.261
We have these two, what I refer to, as air
conditions at the north pole and the south

00:53:12.261 --> 00:53:19.349
pole which moderates our climate and makes
this planet available for human beings to

00:53:19.349 --> 00:53:20.480
exist.

00:53:20.480 --> 00:53:22.260
What happens if they go away?

00:53:22.260 --> 00:53:28.569
And the fact of the matter is every bit of
data we have tell us they're going away.

00:53:28.569 --> 00:53:34.310
I'm kind of scared what that might mean for
our planet.

00:53:34.310 --> 00:53:38.460
I mentioned earlier that human beings have
been looking for shorter sea routes and a

00:53:38.460 --> 00:53:44.819
way across the Arctic for 500 years or more.

00:53:44.819 --> 00:53:46.000
We're finding it now.

00:53:46.000 --> 00:53:49.000
And the entrepreneurs are taking advantage
of it.

00:53:49.000 --> 00:53:56.440
But we're finding it for the wrong reasons,
because that ice is shrinking and ocean currents

00:53:56.440 --> 00:54:01.140
are changing, sea levels are rising, and the
red light has gone on.

00:54:01.140 --> 00:54:03.300
And we need to do something about it, and
I think we can.

00:54:03.300 --> 00:54:04.300
BORGERSON: Thanks.

00:54:04.300 --> 00:54:09.930
BRZEZINSKI: Our self-interest in the Arctic
issue that we all share is the interest of

00:54:09.930 --> 00:54:13.770
our children and our children's children.

00:54:13.770 --> 00:54:21.230
One consistent theme I have found on the Arctic
issue is that we are constantly being shocked

00:54:21.230 --> 00:54:22.690
by broken records.

00:54:22.690 --> 00:54:30.960
This year's the warmest year on record in
the Arctic, the lowest amount of sea ice at

00:54:30.960 --> 00:54:39.510
the high point, since 2015, when records were
broken then, and then since 2014 before that.

00:54:39.510 --> 00:54:47.530
Arctic science at this point is we need a
Pan-Arctic approach to fully understand the

00:54:47.530 --> 00:54:52.640
implications of what is happening there, for
our interests in the rest of the world.

00:54:52.640 --> 00:54:55.809
What happens in the Arctic doesn't stay in
the Arctic.

00:54:55.809 --> 00:54:58.309
It is the refrigerator for the planet.

00:54:58.309 --> 00:55:03.180
And a balanced approach, thinking about the
people of the Arctic, but how all this pertains

00:55:03.180 --> 00:55:07.119
to the people in the rest of the world is
an absolutely-an imperative now.

00:55:07.119 --> 00:55:11.010
BORGERSON: And I think perfectly appropriate
we give the Alaskan the last word on the dais

00:55:11.010 --> 00:55:12.010
this morning.

00:55:12.010 --> 00:55:18.270
MURKOWSKI: And recognizing your commitment
to timeliness, I will sum it all up by just

00:55:18.270 --> 00:55:21.260
saying: Don't forget the people of the Arctic,
period.

00:55:21.260 --> 00:55:23.150
BORGERSON: Thank you.

00:55:23.150 --> 00:55:24.775
Please thank me in joining our panelists for
their time this morning.

00:55:24.775 --> 00:55:25.775
(Applause.)

00:55:25.775 --> 00:55:26.775
Thank you very much.

00:55:26.775 --> 00:55:27.775
Have a great day.

00:55:27.775 --> 00:55:27.776
(END)

